[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001784","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001784","source_record_id":"rec_001784","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"Self-supervised learning on millions of pre-mRNA sequences improves sequence-based RNA splicing prediction","abstract":"RNA splicing is an important post-transcriptional process of gene expression in eukaryotic organisms. Here, we developed a novel language model, SpliceBERT, pre-trained on the precursor messenger RNA sequences of 72 vertebrates to improve sequence-based modelling of RNA splicing. SpliceBERT is capable of generating embeddings that preserve the evolutionary information of nucleotides and functional characteristics of splice sites. Moreover, the pre-trained model can be utilized to prioritize potential splice-disrupting variants in an unsupervised manner based on genetic variants' impact on the output of SpliceBERT for sequence context. Benchmarked on a multi-species splice site and a human branchpoint prediction task, SpliceBERT outperformed not only conventional baseline models but also other language models pretrained only on the human genome. Our study highlighted the importance of unsupervised learning with genomic sequences of multiple species and indicated that language models were promising approaches to decipher the determinants of RNA splicing.","doi":"10.1101/2023.01.31.526427","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: ABSTRACT]\nRNA splicing is an important post-transcriptional process of gene expression in eukaryotic organisms. Here, we developed a novel language model, SpliceBERT, pre-trained on the precursor messenger RNA sequences of 72 vertebrates to improve sequence-based modelling of RNA splicing. SpliceBERT is capable of generating embeddings that preserve the evolutionary information of nucleotides and functional characteristics of splice sites. Moreover, the pre-trained model can be utilized to prioritize potential splice-disrupting variants in an unsupervised manner based on genetic variants' impact on the output of SpliceBERT for sequence context. Benchmarked on a multi-species splice site and a human branchpoint prediction task, SpliceBERT outperformed not only conventional baseline models but also other language models pretrained only on the human genome. Our study highlighted the importance of unsupervised learning with genomic sequences of multiple species and indicated that language models were promising approaches to decipher the determinants of RNA splicing.\n\n[introduction: INTRODUCTION]\nRibonucleic acids (RNA) splicing is a fundamental post-transcriptional process in eukaryotic gene expression, which removes introns from precursor messenger RNAs (pre-mRNAs) and ligates exons into mature mRNAs. Though the mechanism underlying RNA splicing is complex, a variety of studies have found that some key determinants of splicing are encoded in DNA sequences (1-3). Therefore, deciphering splicing codes from RNA sequences by computational models is a promising approach and will facilitate the interpretation of genetic variants affecting RNA splicing.\n\nEarly studies mainly aimed to identify short sequence motifs related to splicing, including exonic splicing enhancers (4), branchpoints (5) and other splicing factors (6) with statistical models. Benefiting from accumulated high-throughput sequencing data, other studies were able to employ more advanced machine learning and deep learning models to directly predict RNA splicing events from sequences. For example, HAL (7) is a model trained on alternative splicing events from millions of random sequences for predicting the change of exon skipping and 5' alternative splicing induced by genetic variations. SPANR (3) is a Bayesian network model for predicting the Percent Spliced In (PSI or Ψ ) of alternatively spliced exons. More recently, deep learning models like MMSplice (8), SpliceAI (9) and Pangolin (10) employed deep convolutional neural networks (CNN) to predict alternative splicing events, splice sites or splice site usage. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: DISCUSSION]\nGenetic information, encoded in the genomes, provided a unique challenge for language models. We are only at the very beginning of unveiling the great learning potential of language models in solving computational genomic problems. Unlike human languages, genomic sequences are extremely long and the nucleotide composition in different functional loci may have completely different characteristics (e.g., different types of repetitive regions), which may bring critical challenges to directly apply the models for human languages successfully. Therefore, in this study, we focused pre-mRNAs and developed SpliceBERT pre-trained on vertebrate pre-mRNA sequences to facilitate the studies of RNA splicing. We were particular interest in how to connect pre-trained models and biological knowledge to give explainable evidence why pre-trained LLMs could improve sequence-based RNA prediction.\n\nMultiple sequence alignment of genome/protein sequences across multiple species has shown a substantial capacity to improve sequencebased predictions because it could capture evolutionary information hidden in the sequences. In our study, we found that SpliceBERT pretrained on multiple species exhibited superior power to single-species DNA/RNA language models in preserving the evolutionary information in nucleotide embeddings. It suggested that although SpliceBERT did not explicitly perform MSA during pre-training, the MLM task may also play a role similar to MSA to obtain evolutionary information. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nWe developed SpliceBERT, a pre-trained language model for pre-mRNA sequences to study and improve sequence-based prediction of RNA splicing. SpliceBERT successfully captured evolutionary information and RNA splicing signals from primary sequences by unsupervised learning. Downstream tasks further demonstrated that our model outperformed the other language models pre-trained on only human data. Our study provided a better understanding of the possible mechanisms by which pre-trained models could improve sequencebased predictions of RNA splicing.","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"ABSTRACT","heading_level":4,"line_start":12,"line_end":15,"text":"RNA splicing is an important post-transcriptional process of gene expression in eukaryotic organisms. Here, we developed a novel language model, SpliceBERT, pre-trained on the precursor messenger RNA sequences of 72 vertebrates to improve sequence-based modelling of RNA splicing. SpliceBERT is capable of generating embeddings that preserve the evolutionary information of nucleotides and functional characteristics of splice sites. Moreover, the pre-trained model can be utilized to prioritize potential splice-disrupting variants in an unsupervised manner based on genetic variants' impact on the output of SpliceBERT for sequence context. Benchmarked on a multi-species splice site and a human branchpoint prediction task, SpliceBERT outperformed not only conventional baseline models but also other language models pretrained only on the human genome. Our study highlighted the importance of unsupervised learning with genomic sequences of multiple species and indicated that language models were promising approaches to decipher the determinants of RNA splicing.","original_chars":1068},{"section_type":"introduction","heading":"INTRODUCTION","heading_level":3,"line_start":16,"line_end":25,"text":"Ribonucleic acids (RNA) splicing is a fundamental post-transcriptional process in eukaryotic gene expression, which removes introns from precursor messenger RNAs (pre-mRNAs) and ligates exons into mature mRNAs. Though the mechanism underlying RNA splicing is complex, a variety of studies have found that some key determinants of splicing are encoded in DNA sequences (1-3). Therefore, deciphering splicing codes from RNA sequences by computational models is a promising approach and will facilitate the interpretation of genetic variants affecting RNA splicing.\n\nEarly studies mainly aimed to identify short sequence motifs related to splicing, including exonic splicing enhancers (4), branchpoints (5) and other splicing factors (6) with statistical models. Benefiting from accumulated high-throughput sequencing data, other studies were able to employ more advanced machine learning and deep learning models to directly predict RNA splicing events from sequences. For example, HAL (7) is a model trained on alternative splicing events from millions of random sequences for predicting the change of exon skipping and 5' alternative splicing induced by genetic variations. SPANR (3) is a Bayesian network model for predicting the Percent Spliced In (PSI or Ψ ) of alternatively spliced exons. More recently, deep learning models like MMSplice (8), SpliceAI (9) and Pangolin (10) employed deep convolutional neural networks (CNN) to predict alternative splicing events, splice sites or splice site usage. [truncated]","original_chars":4794},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"DISCUSSION","heading_level":3,"line_start":138,"line_end":149,"text":"Genetic information, encoded in the genomes, provided a unique challenge for language models. We are only at the very beginning of unveiling the great learning potential of language models in solving computational genomic problems. Unlike human languages, genomic sequences are extremely long and the nucleotide composition in different functional loci may have completely different characteristics (e.g., different types of repetitive regions), which may bring critical challenges to directly apply the models for human languages successfully. Therefore, in this study, we focused pre-mRNAs and developed SpliceBERT pre-trained on vertebrate pre-mRNA sequences to facilitate the studies of RNA splicing. We were particular interest in how to connect pre-trained models and biological knowledge to give explainable evidence why pre-trained LLMs could improve sequence-based RNA prediction.\n\nMultiple sequence alignment of genome/protein sequences across multiple species has shown a substantial capacity to improve sequencebased predictions because it could capture evolutionary information hidden in the sequences. In our study, we found that SpliceBERT pretrained on multiple species exhibited superior power to single-species DNA/RNA language models in preserving the evolutionary information in nucleotide embeddings. It suggested that although SpliceBERT did not explicitly perform MSA during pre-training, the MLM task may also play a role similar to MSA to obtain evolutionary information. [truncated]","original_chars":4451},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Conclusion","heading_level":3,"line_start":150,"line_end":153,"text":"We developed SpliceBERT, a pre-trained language model for pre-mRNA sequences to study and improve sequence-based prediction of RNA splicing. SpliceBERT successfully captured evolutionary information and RNA splicing signals from primary sequences by unsupervised learning. Downstream tasks further demonstrated that our model outperformed the other language models pre-trained on only human data. Our study provided a better understanding of the possible mechanisms by which pre-trained models could improve sequencebased predictions of RNA splicing.","original_chars":550}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_retry_2026-07-08/markdown/run_rec_001784.md"}]

